{
  "requirement": "`trust_but_verify::outline::OutlineContent` implements\n`trust_but_verify::domain::NodeContents`, making it usable directly as\n`Domain::NodeContent` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content`.\n\nIts `fingerprint()` returns the selected item's retained structural hash and\nscheme under {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`.\nIts `format()` returns the selected source text with recognized credentials\nredacted under {cite}`requirements.sdk.sources.redaction` and\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`.\nBoth operations describe the source snapshot retained by the value.\n`text()` retains the original selected bytes for source inspection.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The two tests marked `requirements.sdk.outlines.node_contents` cover each obligation in the requirement.\n\n**NodeContents implementation.** `OutlineContent` implements `NodeContents` in `src/outline/contents.rs#impl[OutlineContent/NodeContents]`. `OutlineContent` has no inherent `fingerprint()` or `format()`, so the tests in `tests/outline_content.rs` that import `trust_but_verify::domain::NodeContents` and call these methods exercise the trait implementation. The generic bound check in `tests/contracts.rs` is not marked for this requirement and I did not rely on it.\n\n**`fingerprint()` returns the item's retained structural hash and scheme.** `selected_contents_outlive_handles_and_later_file_edits` asserts these:\n- `selected.structural_hash() == selected.fingerprint().value`.\n- `selected.fingerprint_scheme() == selected.fingerprint().scheme`.\n\nThe test selects item index 2, not the root, so the fingerprint comes from the selected item.\n\n**`format()` returns the selected text with credentials redacted.**\n- The same test asserts that `format()` on a selected item returns exactly `\"second\"`. That is the selected item's text, not the whole file's.\n- `formatted_contents_redact_credentials_while_retaining_raw_text_and_spans` uses a GitHub-token-shaped credential. It asserts that:\n  - `format()` contains `[REDACTED:GitHub Token]`.\n  - `format()` does not contain the token.\n  - The line count is preserved.\n  - A selected sub-item's `format()` is also redacted.\n\n**Redaction does not change the fingerprint.** The redaction test builds a second source that differs only in the credential and asserts that the two fingerprints are equal.\n\n**Both operations describe the retained snapshot.** The first test drops the file handle and rewrites the file on disk. It then asserts that:\n- `selected.fingerprint()` still equals the original.\n- `format()` and `text()` are unchanged.\n- `from_id` now fails.\n- `select(\"\")` on the retained value still yields the original root text.\n\n**`text()` retains the original bytes.** The redaction test asserts that `contents.text() == source`, including the raw token, and that a selected item's `text()` still contains the token while its `format()` does not.\n\nThe fingerprint's scheme is checked against the accessor rather than a literal value, but the requirement only asks for the retained scheme. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
  "references": "1c6c762f96d1086f3ccea20a1391ce2c8c8311bcd7e386f26abf818136867ca2",
